WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:02.160
So who's my guest this week?

00:00:02.160 --> 00:00:04.280
It's Nyle DiMarco.

00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:07.860
From America's Top Model to
Oscar nominated film producer,

00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:10.650
he's become an ambassador for deaf pride,

00:00:10.650 --> 00:00:13.300
taking on the hearing
world on his own terms.

00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:14.715
He's fourth generation deaf

00:00:14.715 --> 00:00:17.083
choosing not to communicate vocally

00:00:17.083 --> 00:00:20.260
and has long challenged the
notion that learning to sign

00:00:20.260 --> 00:00:22.497
can hinder the ability to speak English.

00:00:22.497 --> 00:00:25.650
His memoir "Deaf Utopia" is a love letter

00:00:25.650 --> 00:00:27.113
to American sign language,

00:00:27.113 --> 00:00:30.883
explaining why he doesn't
think deafness needs fixing.

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:35.650
You're going to hear why competing

00:00:35.650 --> 00:00:38.367
in America's Next Top Model was "hell"

00:00:38.367 --> 00:00:41.760
and why he's pretty
annoyed with Will Smith.

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:43.160
Did you see the slap?

00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:45.510
We're talking about Will
Smith of course, here.

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:49.220
Oh, I did in fact see it all.

00:00:49.220 --> 00:00:50.650
I mean, I think everyone in the audience

00:00:50.650 --> 00:00:52.120
was quite confused initially.

00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:56.140
It seemed like a joke
or like a scripted bit.

00:00:56.140 --> 00:00:56.973
But no.

00:00:56.973 --> 00:00:58.870
As soon as Will Smith started screaming,

00:00:58.870 --> 00:01:00.796
my interpreter let me know

00:01:00.796 --> 00:01:02.977
and everybody went quiet and I thought,

00:01:02.977 --> 00:01:04.710
"What are you doing
stealing our spotlight?

00:01:04.710 --> 00:01:05.620
Come on Will."

00:01:05.620 --> 00:01:06.560
Yeah, I was gonna say.

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:07.393
That must have been,

00:01:07.393 --> 00:01:09.110
I mean, were you actually
annoyed about that?

00:01:09.110 --> 00:01:11.344
Was anyone frustrated
in the deaf community?

00:01:11.344 --> 00:01:14.033
Because it really was the thing.

00:01:15.460 --> 00:01:16.810
Yeah, certainly.

00:01:16.810 --> 00:01:18.210
Yeah, it was my first thought.

00:01:18.210 --> 00:01:19.120
I felt like, you know,

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:20.480
this is such a historic moment

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:21.957
and it really felt like a moment for us.

00:01:21.957 --> 00:01:25.680
And I thought, "At least
let us have this one night."

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:28.840
But you know, it's very
typical for hearing people

00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:31.740
to wanna center themselves
and ask us to just orbit them.

00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:48.890
Nyle DiMarco, welcome to the program.

00:01:48.890 --> 00:01:50.320
It's really good to have you on the show.

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:51.153
And I should say,

00:01:51.153 --> 00:01:52.360
we'll be speaking to each other

00:01:52.360 --> 00:01:54.610
through an interpreter today.

00:01:54.610 --> 00:01:56.524
But what I wanted to start by asking you,

00:01:56.524 --> 00:01:58.400
right at the heart of your book,

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:02.340
the title no less is
what would a deaf utopia

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:03.423
look like to you?

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:08.210
Good morning, a deaf utopia I think for me

00:02:08.210 --> 00:02:09.850
would certainly look like a place where

00:02:09.850 --> 00:02:12.640
we all have complete access.

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:15.580
Which means a world
inclusive of sign language,

00:02:15.580 --> 00:02:19.150
but also with accessible features

00:02:19.150 --> 00:02:20.710
that make deaf people feel like we

00:02:20.710 --> 00:02:23.514
can really connect and
belong on this planet.

00:02:23.514 --> 00:02:24.370
What does that mean?

00:02:24.370 --> 00:02:25.350
I suppose what I'm asking is

00:02:25.350 --> 00:02:27.213
what don't we have at the moment?

00:02:28.370 --> 00:02:29.740
Well, I'd say some of the things

00:02:29.740 --> 00:02:32.070
that we don't have just in general

00:02:32.070 --> 00:02:34.910
is a lack of deaf culture awareness.

00:02:34.910 --> 00:02:37.170
So many people that I meet who are hearing

00:02:37.170 --> 00:02:38.900
in conversation often remind me

00:02:38.900 --> 00:02:41.580
that they don't know
that deaf culture exists.

00:02:41.580 --> 00:02:43.097
They don't really realize
that we have a community

00:02:43.097 --> 00:02:44.475
and from their perspective,

00:02:44.475 --> 00:02:47.490
deafness is a disability and
couldn't possibly be inclusive

00:02:47.490 --> 00:02:49.680
of a culture, which in fact is not true.

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:52.030
We have a language which
gives rise to culture.

00:02:53.820 --> 00:02:55.940
I think essentially that really starts

00:02:55.940 --> 00:02:58.170
with a lack of awareness.

00:02:58.170 --> 00:03:00.370
You also mentioned there the need for sign

00:03:00.370 --> 00:03:03.220
to be better understood
or better recognized.

00:03:03.220 --> 00:03:05.570
Are you talking about in a deaf utopia,

00:03:05.570 --> 00:03:09.120
everyone would be able to
have some kind of literacy?

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:11.520
Yeah, I mean, essentially
I would love that.

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:13.790
You know, I think that's probably one of

00:03:13.790 --> 00:03:16.461
the biggest goals that we
really have as a community.

00:03:16.461 --> 00:03:19.620
You know, sign language
has incredible benefits

00:03:19.620 --> 00:03:22.310
for hearing people and for
deaf people alike, right?

00:03:22.310 --> 00:03:25.390
In sign language we can
speak and communicate faster

00:03:25.390 --> 00:03:26.290
than in English.

00:03:26.290 --> 00:03:28.700
We can also talk through
windows and underwater,

00:03:28.700 --> 00:03:29.750
how cool is that?

00:03:30.639 --> 00:03:31.650
Yes, it really is.

00:03:31.650 --> 00:03:33.630
I hadn't thought about that at all.

00:03:33.630 --> 00:03:35.700
But yeah, there are
other things I'll come to

00:03:35.700 --> 00:03:37.220
from some of your stories about some of

00:03:37.220 --> 00:03:38.950
the benefits people may not have realized,

00:03:38.950 --> 00:03:40.750
not least having a party at home

00:03:40.750 --> 00:03:43.090
without your mom knowing
because you could just shut

00:03:43.090 --> 00:03:44.819
the door and go for it.

00:03:44.819 --> 00:03:47.912
And I think some of those
examples are amazing.

00:03:49.372 --> 00:03:52.683
Yeah, you know, honestly
we call that #deafgames.

00:03:54.253 --> 00:03:57.010
You know, being from a
deaf family is incredible

00:03:57.010 --> 00:03:58.850
but it's also incredibly rare.

00:03:58.850 --> 00:04:01.540
And I think kids like me tend to abuse it.

00:04:01.540 --> 00:04:04.630
My mom being such a good sleeper
was really helpful for us

00:04:04.630 --> 00:04:05.840
when we wanted to rage

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:07.322
at four or five o'clock in the morning.

00:04:07.322 --> 00:04:08.840
I mean, you mentioned your family.

00:04:08.840 --> 00:04:11.529
You're fourth generation deaf.

00:04:11.529 --> 00:04:14.280
What did that mean
growing up in your family

00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:16.527
with how you learned to communicate

00:04:16.527 --> 00:04:18.943
and the culture around you?

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:21.380
Definitely.

00:04:21.380 --> 00:04:23.810
Being born to an entirely deaf family

00:04:23.810 --> 00:04:25.620
and having deaf parents meant

00:04:25.620 --> 00:04:27.900
that I had access to language day one.

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:29.913
To be honest, I don't really remember

00:04:29.913 --> 00:04:32.607
first learning to sign
like many other people

00:04:32.607 --> 00:04:34.690
don't really remember
their first words either.

00:04:34.690 --> 00:04:36.660
It's something that I
wanted to hear your view on

00:04:36.660 --> 00:04:38.460
because we've just been
through a pandemic.

00:04:38.460 --> 00:04:40.403
I mean, we're still in it in lots of ways

00:04:40.403 --> 00:04:43.700
and people were having to homeschool

00:04:43.700 --> 00:04:45.719
and then I imagine for those children

00:04:45.719 --> 00:04:50.430
didn't have access to the deaf
schools and to their peers,

00:04:50.430 --> 00:04:52.951
to the people that they
were used to being with.

00:04:52.951 --> 00:04:54.390
Absolutely, absolutely.

00:04:54.390 --> 00:04:56.430
And it's a great point.

00:04:56.430 --> 00:04:58.870
It was a very large
problem in the community.

00:04:58.870 --> 00:05:02.850
Deaf schools all over were
faced with an entirely new issue

00:05:02.850 --> 00:05:05.180
of how they bring the
exposure to the parents

00:05:05.180 --> 00:05:07.530
and how do they bring them in home?

00:05:07.530 --> 00:05:10.132
How do they provide access to language

00:05:10.132 --> 00:05:12.570
and continue exposing them to our culture

00:05:12.570 --> 00:05:15.490
and continue to give
them not only exposure to

00:05:15.490 --> 00:05:18.450
their role models, but also
the ability to socialize.

00:05:18.450 --> 00:05:20.540
Having to send those
children home to a place

00:05:20.540 --> 00:05:23.320
where they couldn't practice sign

00:05:23.320 --> 00:05:27.731
was in many ways traumatic
for so many kids out there.

00:05:27.731 --> 00:05:30.960
And an entirely new issue that
we faced with the pandemic,

00:05:30.960 --> 00:05:32.050
you're absolutely right.

00:05:32.050 --> 00:05:33.370
I suppose the other thing as well,

00:05:33.370 --> 00:05:34.750
and I wonder if you had
an experience with this,

00:05:34.750 --> 00:05:36.750
is everyone was wearing masks

00:05:36.750 --> 00:05:38.950
and a lot of people still are.

00:05:38.950 --> 00:05:41.451
Was that difficult for
you during the pandemic?

00:05:41.451 --> 00:05:43.618
Definitely, definitely.

00:05:43.618 --> 00:05:46.260
You know, for me in everyday life,

00:05:46.260 --> 00:05:48.917
I depend on lip reading often, you know?

00:05:48.917 --> 00:05:51.370
And honestly, lip reading is certainly not

00:05:51.370 --> 00:05:53.220
the best way to communicate.

00:05:53.220 --> 00:05:54.670
If you look at any of the statistics,

00:05:54.670 --> 00:05:56.130
you see that even the best of lip readers

00:05:56.130 --> 00:05:57.940
can only catch about 30%.

00:05:57.940 --> 00:06:01.090
But suddenly with a mask, I was at zero.

00:06:01.090 --> 00:06:02.820
So, all of the guess work was gone.

00:06:02.820 --> 00:06:04.140
Of course it was frustrating,

00:06:04.140 --> 00:06:06.380
but I think all of us really
were in this together.

00:06:06.380 --> 00:06:08.450
Yes, well, it's lovely to
be able to see your face.

00:06:10.199 --> 00:06:11.950
Likewise.
Yes

00:06:11.950 --> 00:06:14.150
Happy to take those off, for sure.

00:06:14.150 --> 00:06:16.110
And it's nice to be able
to see people's faces now.

00:06:16.110 --> 00:06:16.943
Yeah, exactly.

00:06:16.943 --> 00:06:18.339
And some of the real expressions.

00:06:18.339 --> 00:06:20.380
I'm obviously minded of the fact

00:06:20.380 --> 00:06:21.920
that a lot of people
really liked your face

00:06:21.920 --> 00:06:24.800
because you won "America's
Next Top Model."

00:06:24.800 --> 00:06:26.870
That's something that we should bring up.

00:06:26.870 --> 00:06:31.650
But I wonder when going onto that program,

00:06:31.650 --> 00:06:34.890
were you thinking about
how it was going to work?

00:06:34.890 --> 00:06:36.140
That you

00:06:36.140 --> 00:06:39.770
don't talk in the sense of
how some people were gonna

00:06:39.770 --> 00:06:42.950
then find it to communicate with you?

00:06:42.950 --> 00:06:45.150
Absolutely, absolutely.

00:06:45.150 --> 00:06:47.937
It was honestly one of the
first things that I considered.

00:06:47.937 --> 00:06:49.930
I remember when "America's Next Top Model"

00:06:49.930 --> 00:06:51.770
first reached out to me for the audition.

00:06:51.770 --> 00:06:53.280
I ignored it.

00:06:53.280 --> 00:06:56.640
You know, of course, any
type of reality TV show

00:06:56.640 --> 00:06:57.950
and certainly a competition,

00:06:57.950 --> 00:07:00.390
I felt like wasn't going to
be ready for a deaf person.

00:07:00.390 --> 00:07:02.730
I certainly had never
seen one on TV before.

00:07:02.730 --> 00:07:04.580
But, you know, they
reached out multiple times

00:07:04.580 --> 00:07:05.747
and finally I responded and said,

00:07:05.747 --> 00:07:07.550
"Are you really ready
for a deaf contestant

00:07:07.550 --> 00:07:09.120
in the first place?"

00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:10.790
They said, "Yes."

00:07:10.790 --> 00:07:14.350
So, I started the journey,
but of course it was tough.

00:07:14.350 --> 00:07:17.610
I did have an interpreter on
set, but only 10% of the time.

00:07:17.610 --> 00:07:19.750
Meaning 90% of the time I was lacking

00:07:19.750 --> 00:07:21.688
the ability to really communicate fully.

00:07:21.688 --> 00:07:23.300
I don't think they really understood

00:07:23.300 --> 00:07:26.160
the accessibility needs
that really came along

00:07:26.160 --> 00:07:28.660
with a deaf contestant, but
also how to accommodate.

00:07:28.660 --> 00:07:30.750
And so much of it just
comes with, you know,

00:07:30.750 --> 00:07:34.070
reality TV shows the, you
know, psychological warfare,

00:07:34.070 --> 00:07:35.220
the mind games, you know,

00:07:35.220 --> 00:07:37.130
the causing drama to make better TV.

00:07:37.130 --> 00:07:38.520
And I certainly get that perspective.

00:07:38.520 --> 00:07:41.510
But I survived through four months of it.

00:07:41.510 --> 00:07:43.210
And would I do it again?

00:07:43.210 --> 00:07:45.370
No, I'd rather not walk through hell.

00:07:45.370 --> 00:07:46.203
Not walk through hell?

00:07:46.203 --> 00:07:47.036
Why hell?

00:07:48.340 --> 00:07:50.000
Well, I'll tell you.

00:07:50.000 --> 00:07:52.887
Four months, I mean,
imagine for four months

00:07:52.887 --> 00:07:55.189
you could only use,

00:07:55.189 --> 00:07:58.660
say spoken English for 10% of the time.

00:07:58.660 --> 00:08:01.090
I was texting back and
forth with everybody else,

00:08:01.090 --> 00:08:02.270
which is incredibly limited.

00:08:02.270 --> 00:08:04.240
I mean, think about how
fast we can text, right?

00:08:04.240 --> 00:08:07.520
It doesn't really carry
like a conversation.

00:08:07.520 --> 00:08:09.530
So to only be able to use
your language about 10%

00:08:09.530 --> 00:08:10.540
of the time, you know,

00:08:10.540 --> 00:08:12.980
your language that gives
you not only dignity,

00:08:12.980 --> 00:08:14.490
but who you are.

00:08:14.490 --> 00:08:16.690
It's, you know, your self-confidence,

00:08:16.690 --> 00:08:18.520
your pride and your ability to express.

00:08:18.520 --> 00:08:20.457
I think that the minute you
start to think about that

00:08:20.457 --> 00:08:22.880
you would feel incredibly isolated if

00:08:22.880 --> 00:08:24.850
that had been your experience.

00:08:24.850 --> 00:08:28.361
And yet you could also
be forgiven to think

00:08:28.361 --> 00:08:31.760
being a model is perhaps
a good profession in

00:08:31.760 --> 00:08:33.270
the sense of at least
you don't have to talk

00:08:33.270 --> 00:08:35.630
when you're doing the modeling part.

00:08:35.630 --> 00:08:36.990
Right, right.

00:08:36.990 --> 00:08:37.823
Exactly.

00:08:39.250 --> 00:08:40.641
And to be honest with you,

00:08:40.641 --> 00:08:42.330
it was an incredibly isolating experience.

00:08:42.330 --> 00:08:47.330
But I think in the end, I really
was planning for a result.

00:08:47.750 --> 00:08:50.310
I was looking forward to the end

00:08:50.310 --> 00:08:52.010
and not only to win for myself,

00:08:52.010 --> 00:08:54.120
but also to win for the community

00:08:54.120 --> 00:08:57.010
and to be able to elevate
the amount of awareness

00:08:57.010 --> 00:08:59.070
of our culture and our community.

00:08:59.070 --> 00:09:01.560
Which I think really was more important.

00:09:01.560 --> 00:09:04.370
You know, honestly,
modeling as a deaf person,

00:09:04.370 --> 00:09:06.460
there's a lot of benefits, right?

00:09:06.460 --> 00:09:08.420
I come from a very visual culture

00:09:08.420 --> 00:09:10.290
where we study body language.

00:09:10.290 --> 00:09:12.810
So it's definitely a different level

00:09:12.810 --> 00:09:14.210
being able to study a photographer

00:09:14.210 --> 00:09:15.200
and what they're looking for

00:09:15.200 --> 00:09:17.740
and also to be able to emulate the poses.

00:09:17.740 --> 00:09:20.740
I mean, body language is
really what we're experts at.

00:09:20.740 --> 00:09:23.240
Yeah, you know, I'm
now just thinking about

00:09:23.240 --> 00:09:25.470
how I'm sitting, what I'm
giving off to you here.

00:09:25.470 --> 00:09:27.050
I'm gonna think this through a bit more,

00:09:27.050 --> 00:09:28.390
but it's one of those things

00:09:28.390 --> 00:09:31.300
that it must be a bit of a superpower.

00:09:31.300 --> 00:09:32.390
Oh, absolutely.

00:09:32.390 --> 00:09:33.240
Absolutely.

00:09:33.240 --> 00:09:36.465
And if you take a look at
any of the articles out there

00:09:36.465 --> 00:09:38.660
about deaf people's peripheral vision,

00:09:38.660 --> 00:09:40.960
you'll actually see that
studies have been done to show

00:09:40.960 --> 00:09:43.210
that it's enhanced, it's widened.

00:09:43.210 --> 00:09:45.900
We actually see more and we take more of

00:09:45.900 --> 00:09:47.040
the world in than hearing people.

00:09:47.040 --> 00:09:49.350
So yeah, we're superheroes.

00:09:49.350 --> 00:09:50.400
You'll take that, I'm sure.

00:09:50.400 --> 00:09:52.680
And it's something, you know, again,

00:09:52.680 --> 00:09:54.453
to think about a different
way of seeing the world

00:09:54.453 --> 00:09:58.100
and actually reading the
world while you're in it.

00:09:58.100 --> 00:10:01.140
You also of course, are known
for "Dancing with the Stars"

00:10:01.140 --> 00:10:03.730
and music it seems, again,

00:10:03.730 --> 00:10:06.500
if you haven't thought
about the deaf world,

00:10:06.500 --> 00:10:09.770
the deaf culture, is an
incredibly important part

00:10:09.770 --> 00:10:11.670
of being in the world.

00:10:11.670 --> 00:10:15.620
And it's that experience I
understand of feeling the beat.

00:10:15.620 --> 00:10:17.740
Well, I mean, personally, do I feel,

00:10:18.590 --> 00:10:20.290
if you're asking if I
could feel the beat on set?

00:10:20.290 --> 00:10:21.123
I'll be honest.

00:10:21.123 --> 00:10:23.360
I couldn't hear or feel anything.

00:10:23.360 --> 00:10:26.070
So, our rehearsals were
pretty interesting in that

00:10:26.070 --> 00:10:28.420
we had to practice using other systems.

00:10:28.420 --> 00:10:32.740
We would often use larger studios
that were quite wide open.

00:10:32.740 --> 00:10:36.983
And the system that Peta and
I developed was counting.

00:10:36.983 --> 00:10:38.870
It was just simple math.

00:10:38.870 --> 00:10:41.810
But we also shared a cue
system that we developed.

00:10:41.810 --> 00:10:44.250
For example, if Peta taps me,

00:10:44.250 --> 00:10:46.360
that was say to begin the routine.

00:10:46.360 --> 00:10:47.630
A hand squeeze would mean

00:10:47.630 --> 00:10:51.500
that we were going to switch
specific formation or footwork.

00:10:51.500 --> 00:10:53.850
Scratch on my back might
have meant that I was late.

00:10:53.850 --> 00:10:57.450
So to the audience at home,
of course, nobody noticed.

00:10:57.450 --> 00:10:59.810
But if you go back and
watch some of the reruns,

00:10:59.810 --> 00:11:01.170
you'll be able to catch
some of those cues.

00:11:01.170 --> 00:11:02.003
But for some people

00:11:02.003 --> 00:11:04.370
they do talk about being
able to feel some of

00:11:04.370 --> 00:11:07.000
the beat but you couldn't feel anything.

00:11:07.000 --> 00:11:10.070
Right. It really just
depends on where you are.

00:11:10.070 --> 00:11:13.580
So my twin brother actually,
who is also deaf, is a DJ.

00:11:13.580 --> 00:11:16.150
If you can believe, has an amazing

00:11:16.150 --> 00:11:17.810
brilliant professional setup.

00:11:17.810 --> 00:11:20.950
That's like something like 2000 Watts.

00:11:20.950 --> 00:11:22.670
It's absolutely insane.

00:11:22.670 --> 00:11:24.730
But on dancing with the stars,

00:11:24.730 --> 00:11:27.680
I couldn't feel anything like
what he can in the DJ booth.

00:11:27.680 --> 00:11:28.560
But you know,

00:11:28.560 --> 00:11:32.880
there was a moment certainly
where we had a couple NFL

00:11:32.880 --> 00:11:34.510
dancers who came on,
which was really great.

00:11:34.510 --> 00:11:38.240
And the funny thing was, they
couldn't catch their footwork.

00:11:38.240 --> 00:11:40.610
Right. They just couldn't
get the timing right.

00:11:40.610 --> 00:11:43.340
And at the end, I couldn't hear any of it.

00:11:43.340 --> 00:11:44.370
I couldn't feel any of it.

00:11:44.370 --> 00:11:47.270
And I was still able to keep
the math perfectly on time,

00:11:47.270 --> 00:11:48.540
which is kind of ironic. Right.

00:11:48.540 --> 00:11:50.390
I'm the only one in the
room who can't hear.

00:11:50.390 --> 00:11:51.701
And yet I'm matching.

00:11:51.701 --> 00:11:54.850
Yeah. Well, congratulations
belatedly for, for that,

00:11:54.850 --> 00:11:57.050
because it was obviously
a huge success as well,

00:11:57.050 --> 00:12:00.070
but I suppose what's been
driving your work as well to this

00:12:00.070 --> 00:12:02.800
point because you've been working
now as a producer as well.

00:12:02.800 --> 00:12:04.060
Oscar nominated,

00:12:04.060 --> 00:12:07.750
congratulations for "Audible" on Netflix.

00:12:07.750 --> 00:12:08.583
Thank you very much.

00:12:08.583 --> 00:12:09.680
I mean, that must have
been quite the night,

00:12:09.680 --> 00:12:12.348
especially because of
"CODA" as well, winning.

00:12:12.348 --> 00:12:14.460
Oh, it was incredible.

00:12:14.460 --> 00:12:15.420
It was incredible.

00:12:15.420 --> 00:12:19.340
And truly a watershed
moment for our community.

00:12:19.340 --> 00:12:22.180
If you consider that it's
been 35 years since we've had

00:12:22.180 --> 00:12:24.010
another member of the deaf community

00:12:24.010 --> 00:12:25.960
take to the Oscar stage,

00:12:25.960 --> 00:12:27.143
it's really incredible.

00:12:28.090 --> 00:12:30.130
Marlee Matlin's work in this industry.

00:12:30.130 --> 00:12:31.710
I mean, really, truly speaks for itself.

00:12:31.710 --> 00:12:35.700
But that night we had over 10
deaf people on the red carpet.

00:12:35.700 --> 00:12:38.970
It was incredible to see that
amount of representation.

00:12:38.970 --> 00:12:40.740
And the wins that night, I mean,

00:12:40.740 --> 00:12:43.960
if you consider three
wins alone from "CODA"

00:12:43.960 --> 00:12:45.897
was really groundbreaking.

00:12:45.897 --> 00:12:48.960
And I think it shows that the time is now,

00:12:48.960 --> 00:12:50.620
it really feels that people,

00:12:50.620 --> 00:12:51.870
it really feels like people at home

00:12:51.870 --> 00:12:54.410
are really hungry for our stories.

00:12:54.410 --> 00:12:57.180
And we're really looking
to make this a movement

00:12:57.180 --> 00:12:58.283
rather than a moment.

00:12:59.240 --> 00:13:02.160
I think right now we have to
start bringing deaf people in

00:13:02.160 --> 00:13:02.993
behind the camera.

00:13:02.993 --> 00:13:03.826
Right?

00:13:03.826 --> 00:13:06.520
Which is so key to authenticity
because that truly is where

00:13:06.520 --> 00:13:07.420
it begins.

00:13:07.420 --> 00:13:08.253
Yes.

00:13:08.253 --> 00:13:10.960
Although the Oscars this
year also bring to mind

00:13:10.960 --> 00:13:11.793
something else.

00:13:11.793 --> 00:13:14.240
I was wondering, did you,
did you see the slap?

00:13:14.240 --> 00:13:17.180
We're talking about Will
Smith of course here.

00:13:17.180 --> 00:13:18.050
Oh, I did.

00:13:18.050 --> 00:13:19.486
In fact see it all.

00:13:19.486 --> 00:13:20.319
I mean,

00:13:20.319 --> 00:13:22.070
I think everyone in the
audience was quite confused

00:13:22.070 --> 00:13:26.760
initially. It seemed like a
joke or like a scripted bit,

00:13:26.760 --> 00:13:29.500
but no, soon as Will
Smith started screaming,

00:13:29.500 --> 00:13:30.357
my interpreter let me know.

00:13:30.357 --> 00:13:33.597
And everybody went quiet and I thought,

00:13:33.597 --> 00:13:35.730
"What are you doing stealing
our spotlight? Come on Will."

00:13:35.730 --> 00:13:38.280
Yeah, I was gonna say, that
must have been, I mean,

00:13:38.280 --> 00:13:39.680
were you actually annoyed about that?

00:13:39.680 --> 00:13:41.400
Was anyone frustrated in the,

00:13:41.400 --> 00:13:45.470
the deaf community because
it really was the thing.

00:13:45.470 --> 00:13:47.330
Yeah, certainly it was, yeah, it was my,

00:13:47.330 --> 00:13:48.696
it was my first thought.
I felt like, you know,

00:13:48.696 --> 00:13:51.380
this is such a historic moment
and it really felt like a

00:13:51.380 --> 00:13:52.213
moment for us.

00:13:52.213 --> 00:13:55.670
And I thought, "At least
let us have this one night."

00:13:55.670 --> 00:13:58.930
But you know, it's very
typical for hearing people

00:13:58.930 --> 00:14:02.299
to want to center themselves
and ask us to just orbit them.

00:14:02.299 --> 00:14:03.132
Yeah.

00:14:03.132 --> 00:14:07.280
Although I'm also now trying
to imagine your interpreter

00:14:07.280 --> 00:14:09.790
telling you what Will Smith was saying

00:14:09.790 --> 00:14:11.640
and you getting that as well,

00:14:11.640 --> 00:14:13.872
because if you could hear
it and see it, it was,

00:14:13.872 --> 00:14:16.660
as you said, you didn't believe it.

00:14:16.660 --> 00:14:17.693
No, absolutely.

00:14:18.840 --> 00:14:20.680
Absolutely. So during the Oscars,

00:14:20.680 --> 00:14:23.540
they had two large TV
screens on both sides,

00:14:23.540 --> 00:14:25.210
And so I was able to see Will.

00:14:25.210 --> 00:14:28.230
Because they had panned over
to him and I could clearly see

00:14:28.230 --> 00:14:29.670
a reaction of what was going on.

00:14:29.670 --> 00:14:31.200
But when I asked my
interpreter what he was saying,

00:14:31.200 --> 00:14:32.600
it became very evident

00:14:32.600 --> 00:14:34.250
that it was not a scripted bit at all.

00:14:34.250 --> 00:14:36.940
Yeah, and you definitely
didn't need to be able to hear

00:14:36.940 --> 00:14:38.370
to understand the last bit right?

00:14:38.370 --> 00:14:40.240
Talking about body language.

00:14:40.240 --> 00:14:42.810
Right. Definitely, definitely immediately.

00:14:42.810 --> 00:14:44.870
And I could lip read him on
the, on the cameras as well.

00:14:44.870 --> 00:14:46.030
It was loud, clear as day.

00:14:46.030 --> 00:14:46.863
It was, yeah.

00:14:46.863 --> 00:14:47.696
Well, quite the moment.

00:14:47.696 --> 00:14:48.529
But as you say,

00:14:48.529 --> 00:14:51.070
quite the moment for the deaf
community as well that night,

00:14:51.070 --> 00:14:53.620
and I know that you also
feel very passionately.

00:14:53.620 --> 00:14:55.220
You say about people behind the camera,

00:14:55.220 --> 00:14:59.350
but also on the camera, you are
of the view that deaf actors

00:14:59.350 --> 00:15:01.430
should be playing the deaf roles?

00:15:01.430 --> 00:15:02.891
Absolutely.

00:15:02.891 --> 00:15:04.453
Definitely, definitely.

00:15:05.430 --> 00:15:06.263
I mean,

00:15:07.577 --> 00:15:11.100
I don't think that deaf people
only ever have to play deaf

00:15:11.100 --> 00:15:12.150
roles certainly,

00:15:12.150 --> 00:15:15.300
but I think that auditions
should be open to everyone,

00:15:15.300 --> 00:15:17.250
including members of our community.

00:15:17.250 --> 00:15:20.730
And I think we're at a point
where we shouldn't be hiring,

00:15:20.730 --> 00:15:22.845
hearing people to play
a deaf role, certainly.

00:15:22.845 --> 00:15:24.820
So many people, I think,

00:15:24.820 --> 00:15:28.770
assume that it's easy to take
on the role of a deaf person.

00:15:28.770 --> 00:15:31.860
But, can they actually really go in there

00:15:31.860 --> 00:15:32.693
and kill the audition?

00:15:32.693 --> 00:15:33.920
I don't think so, right?

00:15:33.920 --> 00:15:36.300
Often times people are learning
sign language a few minutes

00:15:36.300 --> 00:15:37.950
ahead of time and, you know,

00:15:37.950 --> 00:15:40.500
sign language takes
years and years and years

00:15:40.500 --> 00:15:42.060
to be able to really become fluent in.

00:15:42.060 --> 00:15:45.130
But also there's a cultural
nuance that's really ingrained

00:15:45.130 --> 00:15:46.850
in us that I think really
shows on the screen.

00:15:46.850 --> 00:15:48.350
I had a son a few years ago

00:15:48.350 --> 00:15:50.280
and we went to a baby sign class.

00:15:50.280 --> 00:15:53.210
And the reason the woman had
started the class was because

00:15:53.210 --> 00:15:54.460
her sister was deaf.

00:15:54.460 --> 00:15:56.686
So she had signed her whole life.

00:15:56.686 --> 00:16:00.550
And it was an amazing way to
communicate with your child

00:16:00.550 --> 00:16:03.010
and you know, we are not deaf,

00:16:03.010 --> 00:16:05.300
but I greatly enjoyed the class.

00:16:05.300 --> 00:16:06.820
It's fantastic.

00:16:06.820 --> 00:16:08.050
A lot of people, I don't think,

00:16:08.050 --> 00:16:10.630
really realize that we have a
lot of apps available, right?

00:16:10.630 --> 00:16:13.880
We have the ASL app, which
I was thrilled to work on

00:16:13.880 --> 00:16:15.560
that provides sign language right.

00:16:15.560 --> 00:16:16.620
That can teach you a little bit,

00:16:16.620 --> 00:16:17.660
not only about our language,

00:16:17.660 --> 00:16:20.070
but also culture, technology
really is amazing.

00:16:20.070 --> 00:16:21.050
It really is.

00:16:21.050 --> 00:16:22.473
Just talking about children,

00:16:23.410 --> 00:16:27.610
there is someone you
quote in the book about

00:16:27.610 --> 00:16:32.610
this whole area around
generations and the way that,

00:16:32.700 --> 00:16:35.130
that this is, or shouldn't be talked about

00:16:35.130 --> 00:16:36.160
and has been in the past,

00:16:36.160 --> 00:16:37.820
you quote the view of the man who patented

00:16:37.820 --> 00:16:40.130
the first telephone Alexander Graham Bell,

00:16:40.130 --> 00:16:42.170
who, despite marrying a deaf woman,

00:16:42.170 --> 00:16:44.520
didn't believe deaf people
should marry each other

00:16:44.520 --> 00:16:46.880
because of the risk of deaf children.

00:16:46.880 --> 00:16:50.010
Why did you include that in the book?

00:16:50.010 --> 00:16:51.020
I included it,

00:16:51.020 --> 00:16:54.840
I think because it's important
for hearing people to read

00:16:54.840 --> 00:16:58.990
and really understand that
deaf issues are not new, right?

00:16:58.990 --> 00:17:02.550
That language deprivation
did not arrive recently,

00:17:02.550 --> 00:17:06.860
but this has been centuries
and centuries of oppression

00:17:08.060 --> 00:17:10.810
and deprivation, you know, in my book,

00:17:10.810 --> 00:17:13.633
I really work to outline
the events sort of,

00:17:13.633 --> 00:17:16.340
of our history and bring
those to modern day,

00:17:16.340 --> 00:17:17.920
which I think a lot of
people don't realize.

00:17:17.920 --> 00:17:20.199
But of course what he
was saying, you know, as,

00:17:20.199 --> 00:17:24.250
as horrible and unpalatable
as it is to hear, or to say,

00:17:24.250 --> 00:17:27.110
I should say, certainly
in this was a lot of the,

00:17:27.110 --> 00:17:29.910
the people's view at the
time and may continue to be.

00:17:29.910 --> 00:17:33.480
And I just wondered as you're
fourth generation within a

00:17:33.480 --> 00:17:35.070
family like that, you know, what,

00:17:35.070 --> 00:17:37.620
what is your message on that point,

00:17:37.620 --> 00:17:40.790
and also how has it made
you think about your future?

00:17:40.790 --> 00:17:41.953
I would say to be honest with you, I,

00:17:41.953 --> 00:17:45.050
I would be open to marrying
a deaf or a hearing person.

00:17:45.050 --> 00:17:46.430
Right.

00:17:46.430 --> 00:17:48.110
You know, I think what's really important

00:17:48.110 --> 00:17:49.760
is sort of that connection

00:17:49.760 --> 00:17:52.840
but, you know, I think we
should all trust our kids

00:17:52.840 --> 00:17:56.840
and certainly ourselves to
know that when we fall in love,

00:17:56.840 --> 00:17:58.810
it's, it's right.

00:17:58.810 --> 00:18:02.180
You know, I don't think that
we should certainly, you know,

00:18:02.180 --> 00:18:03.353
I mean, love is not expensive.

00:18:03.353 --> 00:18:07.150
Right? But it's, you know,
available to all of us I think.

00:18:07.150 --> 00:18:08.110
I mean, I suppose that

00:18:08.110 --> 00:18:10.500
the other question there is
around is around children.

00:18:10.500 --> 00:18:11.840
I mean, you may not have got that far

00:18:11.840 --> 00:18:13.780
and I'm not meaning to pry,

00:18:13.780 --> 00:18:17.224
but when you think about, if
you think about such things,

00:18:17.224 --> 00:18:19.513
do you ever think about,

00:18:19.513 --> 00:18:22.700
that their lives and how you
would want their lives to be

00:18:22.700 --> 00:18:25.043
with your own experiences
and with your siblings?

00:18:26.190 --> 00:18:28.150
So I'll tell you, you know,

00:18:28.150 --> 00:18:30.230
a big part of deaf culture
is that we wanna be able

00:18:30.230 --> 00:18:33.090
to pass it on like any
of our cultures. Right?

00:18:33.090 --> 00:18:35.450
We wanna be able to give
our children that sense of

00:18:35.450 --> 00:18:36.640
community.

00:18:36.640 --> 00:18:39.378
You know, I would love to
have a deaf child, but,

00:18:39.378 --> 00:18:40.240
you know,

00:18:40.240 --> 00:18:43.620
I think I would really want my
children to know the culture,

00:18:43.620 --> 00:18:46.880
to know the community, to
share our language, right.

00:18:46.880 --> 00:18:49.350
That beautiful world that I
was lucky enough to grow up in.

00:18:49.350 --> 00:18:51.630
I would, of course, wanna pass that on

00:18:51.630 --> 00:18:53.610
and I would want them to
carry that same torch.

00:18:53.610 --> 00:18:56.076
I recognize this as a
strange question because I,

00:18:56.076 --> 00:18:57.510
I'm not comparing the two,

00:18:57.510 --> 00:18:59.350
but I sometimes I'm an
only child I get asked,

00:18:59.350 --> 00:19:01.120
"Would you have liked siblings?"

00:19:01.120 --> 00:19:03.060
And how was that as if it was awful,

00:19:03.060 --> 00:19:04.480
but it's the only thing I know.

00:19:04.480 --> 00:19:07.230
So I can only say "I'm okay."

00:19:07.230 --> 00:19:10.410
It was great. You know, I was okay.

00:19:10.410 --> 00:19:12.390
But I was gonna ask you,

00:19:12.390 --> 00:19:14.270
because I think people
are curious about this.

00:19:14.270 --> 00:19:17.080
Do you think that it is a
better way to live without

00:19:17.080 --> 00:19:17.913
hearing,

00:19:17.913 --> 00:19:20.610
because you seem to have got
so much from your culture?

00:19:20.610 --> 00:19:22.607
I typically tell people,

00:19:22.607 --> 00:19:25.290
"I truly don't know any
different much like you," right.

00:19:25.290 --> 00:19:26.980
I grew up entirely deaf.

00:19:26.980 --> 00:19:30.940
And so I don't really have a
concept of sound or silence.

00:19:30.940 --> 00:19:32.430
I mean, I can't hear waves,

00:19:32.430 --> 00:19:33.910
but I think they're really beautiful.

00:19:33.910 --> 00:19:36.320
And so I think our interpretations
are just a little bit

00:19:36.320 --> 00:19:38.710
different and our experience
of those are different.

00:19:38.710 --> 00:19:41.097
But I can tell you, you know, I,

00:19:41.097 --> 00:19:43.430
of course would love to meet

00:19:43.430 --> 00:19:45.540
and work with even more deaf people

00:19:45.540 --> 00:19:47.590
who wanna come to the dark side, I guess.

00:19:49.370 --> 00:19:51.525
That's one way of putting it.

00:19:51.525 --> 00:19:52.610
Have you ever, I don't know if you,

00:19:52.610 --> 00:19:53.770
if it is possible for you,

00:19:53.770 --> 00:19:55.800
have you ever been interested in trying

00:19:55.800 --> 00:19:57.873
any of the technology to help you hear?

00:19:58.930 --> 00:20:00.910
Well, funny enough, a
lot of people don't know.

00:20:00.910 --> 00:20:03.449
I actually used hearing aids growing up.

00:20:03.449 --> 00:20:06.400
But, I hated them not gonna lie.

00:20:06.400 --> 00:20:08.440
I absolutely loathed them.

00:20:08.440 --> 00:20:10.770
The sound used to gimme
the worst headaches

00:20:10.770 --> 00:20:14.000
and my brother who's
deaf loves DJing right.

00:20:14.000 --> 00:20:17.180
In his car, he's got
an insane sound system.

00:20:17.180 --> 00:20:21.550
He will crank it so loud to the
point where your entire body

00:20:21.550 --> 00:20:22.720
is shaking.

00:20:22.720 --> 00:20:24.857
And within five, 10 minutes, I'm like,

00:20:24.857 --> 00:20:27.650
"Bro, bro, bro, you gotta cut it out,"

00:20:27.650 --> 00:20:30.780
because it's so unfamiliar
to me that I seriously,

00:20:30.780 --> 00:20:32.420
I end up with a migraine.

00:20:32.420 --> 00:20:33.870
So, but that's just me personally.

00:20:33.870 --> 00:20:35.770
There's a lot of deaf people
out there who I think can

00:20:35.770 --> 00:20:37.340
relate to it and might not be able to.

00:20:37.340 --> 00:20:39.270
How interesting, I had no idea

00:20:39.270 --> 00:20:41.260
that they could give you
a headache, you know,

00:20:41.260 --> 00:20:42.860
in the bid to, to help you hear.

00:20:44.100 --> 00:20:45.630
Yep.

00:20:45.630 --> 00:20:47.780
That's just, I guess how my hearing works.

00:20:47.780 --> 00:20:49.360
It's well, it's, it's
fascinating 'cuz you know,

00:20:49.360 --> 00:20:50.240
there are those videos,

00:20:50.240 --> 00:20:51.120
you will have seen them,

00:20:51.120 --> 00:20:54.410
of a child hearing for
the very first time.

00:20:54.410 --> 00:20:55.243
And you know,

00:20:55.243 --> 00:20:58.210
I suppose, there is that narrative
that then goes along with

00:20:58.210 --> 00:21:01.070
those sorts of videos about
that being a miracle and that

00:21:01.070 --> 00:21:01.903
being the goal.

00:21:03.370 --> 00:21:07.820
I think a lot of people out
there are very misinformed on

00:21:07.820 --> 00:21:09.450
the experience of deaf people, right?

00:21:09.450 --> 00:21:11.570
People love those videos.

00:21:11.570 --> 00:21:14.330
They love 'em, they're
supposed to feel great,

00:21:14.330 --> 00:21:17.970
but it's a few seconds
of another human's life

00:21:17.970 --> 00:21:20.320
if you consider it. Right?

00:21:20.320 --> 00:21:21.880
You know,

00:21:21.880 --> 00:21:24.744
when I consider how I go about
my day and all of the things

00:21:24.744 --> 00:21:28.300
that are required for a baby
to be able to hear for the

00:21:28.300 --> 00:21:30.581
first time, those two things
are very different, right?

00:21:30.581 --> 00:21:33.760
What's important is not the
ability to hear what's important

00:21:33.760 --> 00:21:36.210
is the access to language. Right?

00:21:36.210 --> 00:21:39.320
And those are two very different
things just because you can

00:21:39.320 --> 00:21:42.360
hear something like I could
with hearing aids, doesn't mean

00:21:42.360 --> 00:21:44.810
that I would have access
to language. Right?

00:21:44.810 --> 00:21:46.490
Of course it's cute.

00:21:46.490 --> 00:21:48.967
But I always want to ask people, okay,

00:21:48.967 --> 00:21:51.210
"You have this child who's now have

00:21:51.210 --> 00:21:52.170
who's now had a hearing aid

00:21:52.170 --> 00:21:54.560
or a cochlear implant in,
what sort of resources are you

